  "member_id": "johansson-goran",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B009",
        "laureates": [
          "Carlton M. Caves",
          "H. Jeff Kimble",
          "Roman Schnabel"
        ],
        "citation": "for making squeezed light a controllable quantum resource for precision measurement"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B051",
    "B020"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B009 remains my preference. It joins a fundamental advance—the recognition that quantum fluctuations can be redistributed to improve a chosen measurement—with enduring experimental realization and deployment in precision interferometry. This is not merely a promising quantum technology: it made a basic limit of optical measurement experimentally controllable, and its benefit has been reproduced and used over decades. Caves, Kimble, and Schnabel give a coherent theory-to-experiment attribution, subject to a final historical examination of the experimental lineage.",
    "case_against": "The strongest reservation is attribution. Squeezed-light physics and its translation into large interferometers involved many experimental groups, so we must verify that Kimble and Schnabel are the most defensible representatives and formulate the citation around squeezing itself rather than later gravitational-wave applications. B051 has a more compact attribution and exceptionally clear metrological maturity.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that Caves supplied the foundational quantum-metrology analysis while Kimble and Schnabel represent experimental development and deployment, while noting distributed credit for observatory-scale implementation.",
        "response": "I agree with this framing and take it as a reason to keep the citation centered on controllable squeezed light, not on a particular observatory. The cross-platform experimental maturation is precisely what makes the achievement stronger than a single technical application."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce judges the metrological impact mature but questions whether the proposed experimental names adequately represent the many developers of squeezed-light sources and detectors.",
        "response": "That is the principal issue requiring a documentary attribution review. It does not weaken the underlying discovery, but it could alter the laureate set. On the supplied record, the three names still span the essential conceptual, quantum-optical, and sustained precision-measurement contributions more coherently than a prize assigned to a large collaboration."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth argues that B020 provides an unusually clear two-person theoretical foundation for quantum computation and simulation with controllable atoms.",
        "response": "I regard B020 as a very strong alternative, especially for the clarity of Cirac and Zoller's attribution. Yet its citation encompasses a broad family of proposals and later implementations; B009 more sharply identifies a quantum phenomenon whose operational metrological consequence is already mature."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record supports Kimble and Schnabel as the most appropriate experimental recipients for a squeezing-centered citation.",
      "How sharply to distinguish foundational squeezed-light work from later gravitational-wave-interferometer implementation."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
